What is email sending in Django?
Django provides a built-in email system that lets your application send emails to users.

Real-time examples:
- Registration confirmation emails.
- Password reset links.
- Order confirmation emails.
- Notifications when an application is submitted.
2. How to send an email
Django provides the send_mail() function.
from django.core.mail import send_mailfrom django.http import HttpResponsedef send_welcome_email(request):    send_mail(        subject="Welcome to our website",        message="Your account has been created successfully.",        from_email="admin@example.com",        recipient_list=["user@example.com"],        fail_silently=False,    )    return HttpResponse("Email sent successfully")

Explanation
Parameter	Purpose
subject	Email subject
message	Plain-text email body
from_email	Sender's email address
recipient_list	List of recipient email addresses
fail_silently=False	Raises an exception if sending fails

Important: This code requires email settings to be configured. Otherwise, the email may not reach the recipient.
3. Configure email settings
For development, you can print emails in your terminal instead of sending real emails.

In settings.py:
EMAIL_BACKEND = (    "django.core.mail.backends.console.EmailBackend")
When send_mail() runs, the email contents appear in the terminal.

For actual emails, configure an SMTP server with its host, port, credentials, TLS settings, and sender address. Keep credentials in environment variables rather than hardcoding them.

Here is the exact depth of knowledge you need to be production-ready:

Level 1: The Setup (Settings Configuration)

You need to know how to connect Django to a real email provider (like Gmail, SendGrid, or Mailgun). You don't need to memorize the variable names, but you must know where they go in your settings.py:
• EMAIL_BACKEND: The engine Django uses (e.g., SMTP backend for production, or a Console backend for local testing).
• EMAIL_HOST & EMAIL_PORT: The address of your email provider.
• EMAIL_HOST_USER & EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD: Your secret credentials (stored securely using environment variables).

Level 2: The Core Tool (How to Write the Code)

You only need to feel comfortable with one main function and one main class:
• send_mail() (The Basic Tool): Used 90% of the time for simple text emails (like welcome notes or password resets). You just need to know its four main arguments: Subject, Message, Sender Email, and Recipient List.python
from django.core.mail import send_mail

send_mail(
    "Welcome to our site!",
    "Thanks for signing up.",
    "from@example.com",
    ["user@example.com"],
)
Use code with caution.
• EmailMultiAlternatives (The Advanced Tool): Used when you want to send beautiful HTML templates (with colors, fonts, and log-in buttons) instead of boring plain text.

Level 3: Production Reality (The Speed Trap)

This is the most critical conceptual boundary you must reach.
• The Problem: Sending an email takes a few seconds. If a user clicks "Register" and your Django server tries to send the email right then and there, the user's browser will freeze and spin for 3–5 seconds waiting for a response.
• The Solution: You need to learn the concept of Asynchronous Tasks. Instead of sending the email instantly, Django drops a note in a queue saying "Hey, send this email whenever you can," and immediately loads the next page for the user.
• Tools to know: You should look into Celery or Django Q to handle this queue in real-world apps.


How this works with Django:

1. The user clicks Send Email.
2. JavaScript instantly hides the button and shows "Sending your email... Please wait...".
3. While the user reads that loading text, the browser sends the data to your Django view.
4. Django processes send_mail(), connects to the email server (which takes 2-4 seconds), and sends it.
5. Django returns a success response, and the browser loads the new success page, making the loading text disappear naturally.



Interview-ready answer: “Django provides send_mail() from django.core.mail to send emails. We configure an email backend, specify the subject, message, sender, and recipient list, and then call the function.”
